{
  "patterns": [
    {
      "id": "conditional_workflow_execution_based_on_input_parameters",
      "title": "Conditional Workflow Execution Based on Input Parameters",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to conditionally execute a workflow based on the presence of specific input parameters. It checks for the existence of either '--cmp' or '--samplesheet' parameters and exits with a help message if neither is provided.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When a pipeline needs to support multiple input modes (e.g., single-sample vs. multi-sample processing).",
        "When mandatory parameters must be validated before workflow execution to prevent downstream errors.",
        "When providing user-friendly help messages for complex pipelines with multiple input modes."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "if (!params.containsKey('cmp') && !params.containsKey('samplesheet')){\n    helpMessage()\n    exit 1\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "The `exit 1` statement will terminate the entire Nextflow execution, not just the workflow.",
        "Parameter names are case-sensitive in the `containsKey` check.",
        "This pattern should be placed early in the workflow to fail fast if requirements aren't met.",
        "The help message function must be defined before it's called in the workflow block."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "conditional_process_execution_based_on_optional_parameters",
      "title": "Conditional Process Execution Based on Optional Parameters",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to conditionally execute processes based on the presence of optional parameters. It uses the `optional()` function to check for parameter existence and executes downstream processes only if the required parameters are provided.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When certain processes in a workflow should only run if specific optional parameters are provided by the user.",
        "For workflows with multiple analysis branches where some branches are optional and depend on user input.",
        "To avoid unnecessary computation when certain outputs or analyses are not required."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "if (optional('multi_clustering__reportree__sample_of_interest') && optional('multi_clustering__reportree__report_threshold')) {\n            find_closest(matrix)\n        }\n        if (optional('multi_clustering__reportree__HC_threshold')) {\n          reportree_hc(vcfs, metadata)\n          augur(reportree_hc.out.nwk_hc, reportree_hc.out.metadata_hc, geodata)\n        }",
      "caveats": [
        "The `optional()` function must be defined in the included functions file and should handle cases where the parameter is not provided.",
        "Ensure that the conditions are correctly specified to avoid unintended process execution or skipping.",
        "If the optional parameters are interdependent, the logic should account for all possible combinations to prevent errors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dynamic_vcf_file_handling_using_flatmap_and_collect",
      "title": "Dynamic VCF File Handling Using flatMap and collect",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to dynamically handle a list of VCF files by using `flatMap` to extract the files from a tuple and `collect` to gather them into a list. This is useful when the input is a channel emitting tuples where one element contains the files.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When input data is structured as tuples and you need to extract specific elements (e.g., files) for downstream processing.",
        "For workflows that need to handle variable numbers of input files dynamically.",
        "To simplify the handling of complex input structures by flattening and collecting relevant data."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "vcfs = input.flatMap { it[1] }.collect()",
      "caveats": [
        "The `flatMap` operator assumes that `it[1]` is a collection; if it is not, this will result in an error.",
        "The `collect` operator gathers all elements into a list, which can be memory-intensive for very large datasets.",
        "Ensure that the input channel emits tuples with the expected structure to avoid index out-of-bounds errors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "multi_output_process_with_selective_publishing",
      "title": "Multi-Output Process with Selective Publishing",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how a process can emit multiple outputs and selectively publish them to different directories. It uses the `emit` directive to name specific outputs and `publishDir` with patterns to control which files are published where.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When a process generates multiple outputs that need to be organized into different directories or published under different names.",
        "For workflows that require fine-grained control over which files are published and where.",
        "To separate intermediate files from final results or metadata."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "output:\n      path '*'\n      path 'gt_dist_hamming.tsv', emit: matrix\n      path '{*.sh,*.log}', hidden: true\n    publishDir mode: 'copy', \"${params.outdir}/result\", pattern: 'gt_zooms.txt', saveAs: { \"zooms.txt\" }\n    publishDir mode: 'copy', \"${params.outdir}/result\", pattern: '{*.tsv,*.txt,*.nwk}'\n    publishDir mode: 'copy', \"${params.outdir}/result\", pattern: '*_*[0-9]'\n    publishDir mode: 'rellink', \"${params.outdir}/meta\", pattern: '.command.log', saveAs: { \"reportree_gt.log\" }\n    publishDir mode: 'rellink', \"${params.outdir}/meta\", pattern: '.command.sh', saveAs: { \"reportree_gt.cfg\" }",
      "caveats": [
        "The `emit` directive must be used carefully to avoid naming conflicts with other outputs.",
        "Patterns in `publishDir` must be specific enough to avoid unintended file publishing or exclusion.",
        "Using `hidden: true` for certain outputs can help manage clutter but may make debugging more difficult if logs or scripts are needed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sorted_and_flattened_input_collection",
      "title": "Sorted and Flattened Input Collection",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to process input data by first mapping to extract relevant elements, sorting them based on a specific key, flattening the nested structure, and finally collecting all elements into a single list. This is particularly useful when dealing with nested data structures where order matters and you need a flat list for downstream processing.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When input data is nested and needs to be flattened while maintaining a specific order.",
        "When preprocessing input data to ensure consistent ordering before passing to downstream processes.",
        "When dealing with paired-end reads or other nested data structures that need to be processed in a specific sequence."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "reads = getInput()\n    .map { it[1] }\n    .toSortedList( { a, b -> a[0] <=> b[0] } )\n    .flatten()\n    .collect()",
      "caveats": [
        "The `map` operation assumes that the input structure is consistent and that `it[1]` exists.",
        "The sorting key `a[0]` assumes that the elements are comparable and that the first element is suitable for sorting.",
        "The `flatten()` operation assumes that the sorted list contains nested structures that need to be flattened.",
        "The `collect()` operation will gather all elements into memory, which could be a problem for very large datasets."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "input_tuple_flattening_with_flatmap",
      "title": "Input Tuple Flattening with flatMap",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to handle nested input structures by using `flatMap` to extract specific elements from tuples. In this case, it's extracting the second element (index 1) from each input tuple and collecting them into a list for downstream processing.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When workflow inputs contain nested structures (like tuples) and you need to extract specific elements",
        "Processing multiple files that are grouped together in input channels",
        "When you need to transform complex input structures into simpler ones for downstream processes"
      ],
      "groovy_code": "fastas = input.flatMap { it[1] }.collect()",
      "caveats": [
        "Array indices are zero-based, so `it[1]` refers to the second element - off-by-one errors are common",
        "The `collect()` operator is terminal and will materialize the entire collection in memory - be cautious with large datasets",
        "If the input tuples have variable lengths or missing elements, this will throw an exception",
        "The resulting collection is a List, not a Channel, so it's eagerly evaluated"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "multi_output_process_chaining",
      "title": "Multi-Output Process Chaining",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to handle processes with multiple named outputs and chain them together. The `reportree_hc` process emits two named outputs (`nwk_hc` and `metadata_hc`) which are then both used as inputs to the `augur` process.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When a process produces multiple outputs that need to be used together in downstream processes",
        "Workflows where intermediate results need to be passed through multiple processing steps",
        "Scenarios where you want to maintain clear data flow between processes with multiple outputs"
      ],
      "groovy_code": "reportree_hc(alignment, metadata)\naugur(reportree_hc.out.nwk_hc, reportree_hc.out.metadata_hc, geodata)",
      "caveats": [
        "All named outputs must be properly declared in the process definition with `emit:` labels",
        "The output names must match exactly when accessing them (e.g., `out.nwk_hc` not `out.nwk`)",
        "If any output is missing or not emitted, downstream processes will fail",
        "Processes with multiple outputs can make the workflow harder to debug - consider intermediate validation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tuple_unpacking_with_reference_passing",
      "title": "Tuple Unpacking with Reference Passing",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to unpack a tuple in a process input while passing specific elements to downstream processes. The tuple contains metadata, a reference code, and a reference path, but only the reference path is used in the process, while the entire tuple is passed to maintain data integrity.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to pass a complex data structure (like a tuple) through multiple processes but only use specific elements in each process.",
        "Useful for maintaining data lineage while selectively using parts of the data in different processes.",
        "Ideal for workflows where reference data (like genome references) needs to be passed consistently but only certain attributes are used in each step."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "input:\n  tuple val(_), val(ref_code), path(ref_path)",
      "caveats": [
        "The tuple must be structured consistently across all processes that use it. Any mismatch in the tuple structure will cause errors.",
        "Unused elements in the tuple (like `val(_)`) must still be accounted for to avoid index out-of-bounds errors.",
        "If the tuple structure changes, all processes that depend on it must be updated to reflect the new structure."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "multi_process_data_flow_with_emit",
      "title": "Multi-Process Data Flow with Emit",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to chain multiple processes together, where each process emits specific outputs that are used as inputs to subsequent processes. The `emit` directive is used to explicitly name outputs, which are then referenced in downstream processes.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When building a pipeline where each step depends on the output of the previous step, and you need to explicitly manage the data flow.",
        "Useful for workflows with complex dependencies where intermediate outputs need to be clearly named and referenced.",
        "Ideal for scenarios where you want to avoid ambiguity in data flow by explicitly naming outputs."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "alignment = align(fastas, reference).alignment\ntree_raw = tree(alignment).tree_raw\ntree = refine(tree_raw, alignment, metadata).tree",
      "caveats": [
        "Each process must emit the outputs that are expected by downstream processes. Mismatches in output names or types will cause errors.",
        "The order of process execution is determined by the data dependencies. Circular dependencies or missing outputs will result in workflow failures.",
        "Explicitly naming outputs with `emit` can make the workflow more verbose but improves clarity and maintainability."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "conditional_parameter_handling_with_optional",
      "title": "Conditional Parameter Handling with Optional",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to handle optional parameters in a workflow using a custom `optional` function. The function checks if a parameter is defined and returns it, or returns an empty string if the parameter is not defined. This allows for flexible configuration of process scripts.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to make certain process parameters optional, allowing for more flexible workflow configurations.",
        "Useful for workflows where some parameters are only required in specific scenarios or for advanced users.",
        "Ideal for maintaining backward compatibility when adding new parameters to existing workflows."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "extra = optional('multi_clustering__augur__align_extra')",
      "caveats": [
        "The `optional` function must be defined and imported in the workflow. If not, the workflow will fail.",
        "Optional parameters must be handled carefully in the process script to avoid syntax errors or unexpected behavior.",
        "Overuse of optional parameters can make the workflow configuration complex and harder to debug."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dynamic_metadata_column_mapping",
      "title": "Dynamic Metadata Column Mapping",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to dynamically map metadata columns using parameterized column names. The workflow uses parameters to define which columns in the metadata should be used for specific purposes, such as sample names or dates, allowing for flexible metadata handling.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to work with metadata that has variable column names or structures.",
        "Useful for workflows that need to adapt to different metadata formats without changing the workflow code.",
        "Ideal for scenarios where metadata columns need to be dynamically mapped to specific roles (e.g., sample names, dates)."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "cat ${metadata} | sed 's/${SAMPLE_COLUMN}/name/i' | sed -E 's/${SUMMARY_DATE_ALIASES}/date/i' > augur_metadata.tsv",
      "caveats": [
        "The parameterized column names must match the actual column names in the metadata file. Mismatches will result in incorrect data processing.",
        "The `sed` commands used for column mapping are case-sensitive and rely on exact string matches. Variations in column names (e.g., case differences) will cause issues.",
        "Dynamic column mapping can make the workflow harder to debug, as the actual column names used are not visible in the workflow code."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "channel_aggregation_using_inject",
      "title": "Channel Aggregation Using inject",
      "description": "This pattern uses the `inject` method to aggregate multiple channels into a single channel. It starts with an empty channel and iteratively mixes it with channels generated by the `_getAlleles` function for each input value.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to combine multiple input sources into a single channel for downstream processing.",
        "For workflows that process multiple samples or datasets in parallel.",
        "To aggregate results from multiple upstream processes."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "def getAlleles() {\n    if (!params.containsKey('input')) {\n        exit 2, \"missing required param: input\";\n    }\n    def schema = params.containsKey('schema') ? params.schema : null\n    assert params.input instanceof ArrayList\n    params.input.inject(Channel.empty()) {\n        res, val -> res.mix(_getAlleles(val.cmp, val.riscd, schema))\n    }\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "The `inject` method requires the initial value to be a channel (e.g., `Channel.empty()`).",
        "The `_getAlleles` function must return a channel for the `mix` operator to work correctly.",
        "Be mindful of memory usage when aggregating large channels."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dynamic_parameter_handling_with_optional",
      "title": "Dynamic Parameter Handling with Optional",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to handle optional parameters dynamically, including sanitizing input and constructing command-line arguments conditionally. It uses the `optional` function to check for parameter existence and applies transformations to the parameter values.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When command-line arguments need to be constructed conditionally based on user input.",
        "For sanitizing and formatting parameter values before use in processes.",
        "To handle optional parameters that may or may not be provided by the user."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "soi = optional('multi_clustering__reportree__sample_of_interest').replaceAll(/[\\s\\n\\t\\r\"'${}]/, \"\")\nsoi_par = soi ? \" --sample_of_interest ${soi}\" : ''\nzoom = optional('multi_clustering__reportree__zoom_cluster_of_interest').replaceAll(/[\\s\\n\\t\\r\"'${}]/, \"\")\nzoom_par = zoom ? \" --zoom-cluster-of-interest  ${zoom}\" : ''",
      "caveats": [
        "The `optional` function must be defined in the included functions file.",
        "Regular expressions for sanitizing input must be carefully crafted to avoid unintended modifications.",
        "Ensure that the conditional construction of command-line arguments does not introduce syntax errors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "conditional_publish_dir_with_saveas",
      "title": "Conditional Publish Directory with Dynamic File Renaming",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to conditionally publish files to different directories with dynamic renaming using the `saveAs` directive. It is particularly useful when you need to organize output files into different directories based on their type or when you want to rename files during publishing.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to separate log files, configuration files, and result files into different directories.",
        "When you want to rename output files to more descriptive names during the publishing process.",
        "When you need to apply different publishing modes (e.g., 'copy', 'rellink') to different types of files."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "publishDir mode: 'copy', \"${params.outdir}/result\", pattern: 'tree.nwk'\npublishDir mode: 'rellink', \"${params.outdir}/meta\", pattern: '*.log'\npublishDir mode: 'rellink', \"${params.outdir}/meta\", pattern: '.command.sh', saveAs: { \"vcf2mst.cfg\" }",
      "caveats": [
        "The `pattern` directive must match the files you want to publish. If no files match the pattern, nothing will be published.",
        "The `saveAs` directive is a closure that must return a string. Ensure the closure is correctly formatted to avoid syntax errors.",
        "Different `publishDir` directives for the same process must not overlap in their file patterns to avoid conflicts.",
        "The `mode` directive must be compatible with your storage system. For example, 'rellink' may not work on all file systems."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "workflow_input_transformation_with_map_and_collect",
      "title": "Workflow Input Transformation with Map and Collect",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to transform and collect workflow inputs using the `map` and `collect` operators. It is useful for preprocessing input data before passing it to downstream processes.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to extract specific elements from a list of tuples or complex data structures.",
        "When you want to aggregate multiple input files into a single collection for batch processing.",
        "When you need to preprocess input data (e.g., filtering, transforming) before passing it to a process."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "workflow {\n    getVCFs()\n    .map { it[1] }\n    .collect()\n    .set { inputSet }\n    multi_clustering__vcf2mst(inputSet)\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "The `map` operator must be used with a closure that correctly transforms each element. Incorrect closures can lead to runtime errors.",
        "The `collect` operator will gather all elements into a single list, which can be memory-intensive for large datasets.",
        "Ensure that the transformed data structure matches the expected input format of the downstream process.",
        "The `set` operator is used to assign the transformed data to a variable, which can then be used in the workflow."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "multi_output_process_with_named_emits",
      "title": "Multi-Output Process with Named Emits",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to define a process with multiple output channels, using named emits to distinguish between different types of outputs. It is useful when a process generates multiple types of files that need to be handled separately.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When a process generates multiple types of output files that need to be processed differently.",
        "When you want to explicitly name output channels for better readability and maintainability.",
        "When you need to selectively pass specific outputs to downstream processes."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "process vcf2mst {\n    output:\n      path '*'\n      path 'HDmatrix.tsv', emit: matrix\n      path '*.sh', hidden: true\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "The `emit` directive must be used with a unique name for each named output channel. Duplicate names will cause errors.",
        "The `hidden: true` directive is used to hide certain outputs from the main output channel. Ensure this is intentional to avoid losing track of files.",
        "Named output channels must be accessed using the correct name in the workflow (e.g., `vcf2mst(input).matrix`).",
        "Ensure that the file patterns in the output directives match the actual files generated by the process script."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tuple_unpacking_with_map_and_collect",
      "title": "Tuple Unpacking with Map and Collect",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to unpack tuples from an input channel, extract specific elements using `.map`, and then collect all elements into a list using `.collect()`. This is useful when the input channel emits tuples, but only certain elements are needed for downstream processing.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When the input channel emits tuples, but only specific elements (e.g., file paths) are required for processing.",
        "When you need to aggregate multiple inputs into a single list for batch processing.",
        "When working with workflows that require preprocessing of input data before passing it to a process."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "input\n    .map { it[1] }\n    .collect()\n    .set { gffs }",
      "caveats": [
        "Ensure that the input channel emits tuples with at least two elements; otherwise, `it[1]` will throw an `IndexOutOfBoundsException`.",
        "The `.collect()` operator will buffer all elements in memory, which can be problematic for large datasets. Use with caution in memory-constrained environments.",
        "The `.set` operator is used to assign the collected list to a variable, which is specific to Nextflow DSL2 and may not be available in other Groovy contexts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "conditional_channel_mixing_with_inject",
      "title": "Conditional Channel Mixing with Inject",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to conditionally mix multiple channels into a single channel using the `inject` method. It is particularly useful when you need to dynamically combine channels based on runtime conditions or input parameters.",
      "use_cases": [
        "Combining multiple input sources into a single channel for downstream processing.",
        "Dynamically adding channels based on runtime conditions or parameters.",
        "Aggregating results from multiple processes or data sources into a unified channel."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "def getInput() {\n    if (!params.containsKey('input')) {\n      exit 2, \"missing required param: input\";\n    }\n    def schema = params.containsKey('schema') ? params.schema : null\n    assert params.input instanceof ArrayList\n    params.input.inject(Channel.empty()) {\n        res, val -> res.mix(_getAlleles(val.cmp, val.riscd, schema))\n    }\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "Ensure that the initial channel (`Channel.empty()`) is compatible with the type of channels being mixed.",
        "The `inject` method requires careful handling of the accumulator (`res`) and the current value (`val`).",
        "Make sure that the `_getAlleles` function returns a channel that can be mixed with the accumulator channel.",
        "This pattern can lead to complex debugging if the mixed channels have incompatible data types or structures."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dynamic_parameter_substitution_in_script",
      "title": "Dynamic Parameter Substitution in Script",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to dynamically substitute parameters within a process script using Groovy string interpolation. It is useful for customizing process behavior based on runtime parameters.",
      "use_cases": [
        "Customizing file paths or command-line arguments based on runtime parameters.",
        "Dynamically adjusting script behavior based on input metadata or configuration.",
        "Substituting parameter values into command-line tools or scripts within a process."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "script:\n  \"\"\"\n    cat ${metadata} | sed 's/${SAMPLE_COLUMN}/name/i' \\\n       | sed 's/${SUMMARY_DATE_ALIASES}/date/i' > augur_metadata.tsv\n    METADATA_LIST=\\$(head -n 1 augur_metadata.tsv | tr \\$'\\t' ' ')\n    augur refine --tree ${nwk} --output-tree tree_tt.nwk --output-node-data refine.node.json --metadata augur_metadata.tsv\n    augur export v2 --tree tree_tt.nwk --node-data refine.node.json --output auspice.json \\\n      --color-by-metadata \\${METADATA_LIST} \\\n      --geo-resolutions ${GEO_RESOLUTION_COLUMNS} \\\n      --metadata augur_metadata.tsv \\\n      --lat-longs ${geodata}\n  \"\"\"",
      "caveats": [
        "Ensure that the parameters being substituted are properly defined and accessible within the process scope.",
        "Be cautious with special characters and escaping within the script block to avoid syntax errors.",
        "Dynamic substitution can make scripts harder to debug if parameter values are not as expected.",
        "Ensure that the substituted parameters are compatible with the command-line tools being used."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "conditional_module_inclusion_based_on_parameter",
      "title": "Conditional Module Inclusion Based on Parameter",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to dynamically include different modules and functions based on a parameter value. It allows for flexible workflow configuration where different input types (alleles, alignment, VCF) trigger the inclusion of specific modules tailored to handle those types.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When a workflow needs to handle multiple input types with distinct processing logic.",
        "For creating modular workflows where different data types require different preprocessing steps.",
        "When you want to avoid loading unnecessary modules based on runtime parameters."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "if (getReportreeInputType() == 'alleles') {\n  include { multi_clustering__reportree } from \"../multi/multi_clustering__reportree_alleles\"\n  include { getAlleles as inputFn } from \"../multi/multi_clustering__reportree_alleles\"\n} else if (getReportreeInputType() == 'alignment') {\n  include { multi_clustering__reportree } from \"../multi/multi_clustering__reportree_alignment\"\n  include { getInput as inputFn } from '../functions/parameters.nf'\n} else {\n  include { multi_clustering__reportree } from \"../multi/multi_clustering__reportree_vcf\"\n  include { getVCFs as inputFn } from '../functions/parameters.nf'\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "The parameter validation must be strict to avoid runtime errors due to invalid input types.",
        "Module paths must be correctly specified to avoid file not found errors during inclusion.",
        "The `inputFn` alias must be consistently used in the workflow call to avoid confusion.",
        "Changes in module structure or function names in included modules may break the workflow if not updated accordingly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "parameter_validation_with_early_exit",
      "title": "Parameter Validation with Early Exit",
      "description": "This pattern shows how to validate a parameter against a set of allowed values and exit the workflow with an error message if the validation fails. It ensures that only valid configurations proceed to execution.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When workflow parameters must conform to a specific set of allowed values.",
        "For providing clear error messages to users when invalid parameters are provided.",
        "To enforce strict configuration requirements before workflow execution begins."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "def getReportreeInputType() {\n    def res = param('multi_clustering__reportree__input')\n    if (!(res in ['alleles', 'vcf', 'alignment'])) {\n        exit 2, \"params (multi_clustering__reportree__input) not valid\"\n    }\n    return res\n}",
      "caveats": [
        "The exit code (2 in this case) should be documented and consistent with other error handling in the workflow.",
        "The validation logic must be updated if the set of allowed values changes.",
        "The parameter name in the error message must match exactly to avoid user confusion.",
        "This validation only checks the parameter value, not the existence of the parameter itself."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "optional_parameter_with_default_value_handling",
      "title": "Optional Parameter with Default Value Handling",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to handle optional parameters by providing a default value if the parameter is not specified. It uses a helper function `optionalOrDefault` to simplify the workflow call.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When a workflow parameter is optional and should have a sensible default value.",
        "To reduce complexity in the workflow definition by handling optional parameters in a helper function.",
        "When you want to provide flexibility in parameter specification without breaking the workflow."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "multi_clustering__reportree(inputFn(),  param('metadata'), param('geodata'), optionalOrDefault('multi_clustering__reportree__nomenclature', getEmpty()));",
      "caveats": [
        "The `optionalOrDefault` function must be properly defined to handle cases where the parameter is not provided.",
        "The default value (`getEmpty()` in this case) must be appropriate for the workflow logic.",
        "The order of parameters in the workflow call must match the expected order in the process definition.",
        "Adding or removing optional parameters may require updates to the process definition."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tuple_unpacking_with_conditional_logic",
      "title": "Tuple Unpacking with Conditional Logic",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to unpack a tuple input in a Nextflow process and apply conditional logic based on the type of data (e.g., FASTA, paired Illumina, or Ion Torrent reads). It is useful for handling different types of input data within a single process.",
      "use_cases": [
        "Handling different types of sequencing data (FASTA, paired Illumina, Ion Torrent) in a single process.",
        "Applying different command-line arguments based on the input data type.",
