      "groovy_code": "def getInput() {\n    if (params.containsKey('input')) {\n        assert params.input instanceof ArrayList\n        params.input.inject (Channel.empty()) {\n            res, val -> res.mix(getResult(val.cmp, val.riscd, null))\n        }        \n    } else if (params.containsKey('cmp') && params.containsKey('riscd')) {\n        getResult(params.cmp, params.riscd, null)\n    } else {\n        exit 2, \"missing required params (cmp,riscd) or (input)\";\n    } \n}",
      "caveats": [
        "Ensure that the `inject` method is used correctly to avoid unintended channel behavior.",
        "The use of `mix` should be carefully considered to avoid channel interference."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tuple_unpacking_with_conditional_metadata_processing",
      "title": "Tuple Unpacking with Conditional Metadata Processing",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to unpack a tuple input in a process while conditionally processing metadata derived from one of the tuple elements. The `scaffolds` path is used to parse metadata, which is then combined with execution metadata to generate a unique identifier (`riscd`). This is useful for tracking provenance and ensuring reproducibility.",
      "use_cases": [
        "When you need to dynamically generate metadata from input file names and combine it with execution context.",
        "For workflows requiring detailed provenance tracking where input file names encode experimental metadata.",
        "When you need to emit multiple outputs from a process with different metadata annotations."
