    {
      "id": "conditional_file_processing_with_sed",
      "title": "Conditional File Processing with Sed",
      "description": "This pattern demonstrates how to conditionally process files using sed commands within a Nextflow process. It is particularly useful for transforming file names or content based on specific conditions, such as the presence of certain patterns or the need to standardize naming conventions.",
      "use_cases": [
        "Renaming files based on specific patterns in their names.",
        "Transforming file content to meet specific formatting requirements.",
        "Standardizing file names or content across different datasets."
      ],
      "groovy_code": "extra_params = (analysis_type == 'core' ? '-core' : '')\noutfile = (analysis_type == 'core' ? 'core_SNPs_matrix.fasta' : 'SNPs_all_matrix.fasta')\n\"\"\"\n  trap \"rm -Rf results/TemporaryFilesToDelete\" EXIT\n  for FILE in ${assembly}; do \\\n    CMP=`echo \\$FILE | sed -E 's/DS[[:digit:]]+-DT[[:digit:]]+_([^_]+)_[[:graph:]]+/\\\\1/' | sed 's/\\\\./-/g'`; \\\n    ln -s \\$FILE \\${CMP}.fasta ; \\\n    echo -e \"`pwd`/\\${CMP}.fasta\\t\\$CMP\" >> input.tsv ; \\\n  done        \n  kSNP3 -in input.tsv -k ${kmers_size} -NJ ${extra_params} -outdir results > ksnp3.log\n\"\"\"",
      "caveats": [
        "Ensure that the sed commands are correctly formatted to avoid syntax errors.",
        "Be cautious with file paths and names to prevent unintended file overwrites or deletions.",
        "Test the sed commands thoroughly before applying them to large datasets to ensure they produce the desired transformations."
      ]
    },
    {
